<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="sec-row-reduction" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>The Row Reduction Algorithm</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      We now have all the pieces: elementary row operations preserve the solution set, and
      echelon forms make the solution set easy to read off. The row reduction algorithm
      ties them together into a procedure that solves <em>any</em> system of linear
      equations.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-the-algorithm">
    <title>The Algorithm</title>

    <algorithm xml:id="alg-row-reduction">
      <title>The Row Reduction Algorithm</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <ol>
            <li><p>Starting with the whole matrix, choose the leftmost column containing
            a nonzero entry among the remaining rows. If all remaining rows are zero,
            the forward phase is finished.</p></li>
            <li><p>Interchange rows if necessary to put a nonzero entry of that column
            in the top remaining row. This entry is the next pivot.</p></li>
            <li><p>Use row replacements to create zeros below the pivot.</p></li>
            <li><p>Continue with the rows strictly below the pivot row and the columns
            strictly to the right of the pivot column. Repeat steps 1<ndash/>3 until
            no rows or columns remain, or the remaining rows are zero. The matrix
            is now in row-echelon form.</p></li>
            <li><p>Beginning with the rightmost pivot and working left, divide each
            pivot row by its pivot to make it <m>1</m>, then use row replacements to
            create zeros above it. The result is reduced row-echelon form.</p></li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </algorithm>

    <remark xml:id="rem-forward-backward">
      <p>
        Note that steps 1<ndash />4 will produce a row-echelon matrix, and step 5 will
        convert it to a reduced row-echelon matrix:
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              <term>Steps 1<ndash />4:</term> the <term>forward phase</term>, to obtain
              the REM.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <term>Step 5:</term> the <term>backward phase</term>, to obtain the RREM.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </remark>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-three-outcomes">
    <title>The Three Possible Outcomes</title>

    <p>
      The three graphical possibilities of
      <xref ref="fig-three-possibilities" text="type-global" /> correspond to three
      outcomes of the algorithm. The next three examples illustrate each in turn.
    </p>

    <example xml:id="eg-unique-solution">
      <title>A System with a Unique Solution</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Solve the system
          <md>
            <mrow>
              x + y + 2z \amp = -1
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              y + 2x + 3z \amp = 0
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              z - 2y \amp = 2
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          First rewrite each equation with the variables in the order <m>x, y, z</m> and
          form the augmented matrix. The forward phase gives
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 1 \amp 2 \amp -1 \\
                2 \amp 1 \amp 3 \amp 0 \\
                0 \amp -2 \amp 1 \amp 2
              \end{array} \right]
              \amp \xrightarrow{-2r_1 + r_2}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 1 \amp 2 \amp -1 \\
                0 \amp -1 \amp -1 \amp 2 \\
                0 \amp -2 \amp 1 \amp 2
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{-2r_2 + r_3}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 1 \amp 2 \amp -1 \\
                0 \amp -1 \amp -1 \amp 2 \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 3 \amp -2
              \end{array} \right],
            </mrow>
          </md>
          which is a row-echelon matrix.
        </p>
        <p>
          Now the backward phase:
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{\frac{1}{3} r_3}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 1 \amp 2 \amp -1 \\
                0 \amp -1 \amp -1 \amp 2 \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp -\frac{2}{3}
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow[-2r_3 + r_1]{r_3 + r_2}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp \frac{1}{3} \\
                0 \amp -1 \amp 0 \amp \frac{4}{3} \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp -\frac{2}{3}
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{-r_2}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp \frac{1}{3} \\
                0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp -\frac{4}{3} \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp -\frac{2}{3}
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{-r_2 + r_1}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp \frac{5}{3} \\
                0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp -\frac{4}{3} \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp -\frac{2}{3}
              \end{array} \right],
            </mrow>
          </md>
          a reduced row-echelon matrix.
        </p>
        <p>
          Reading off the rows,
          <md>
            x = \frac{5}{3}, \qquad y = -\frac{4}{3}, \qquad z = -\frac{2}{3},
          </md>
          a unique solution. Every column of the coefficient matrix is a pivot column, so
          there are no free variables.
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-unique-planes">
          <caption>The three planes meet at exactly one point, <m>(5/3,-4/3,-2/3)</m>, the unique solution. The drawing shows finite portions of infinite planes;
          the labels identify the equations in their original order.</caption>
          <image source="images/ch1-unique-planes.svg" width="90%">
            <shortdescription>Three planes have one common point at (5/3,-4/3,-2/3).</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </figure>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="eg-no-solution-rr">
      <title>An Inconsistent System</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Solve the system
          <md>
            <mrow>
              -3x_1 - 9x_2 + x_3 \amp = -9
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              2x_1 + 6x_2 - x_3 \amp = 6
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              x_1 + 3x_2 - x_3 \amp = 2
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                -3 \amp -9 \amp 1 \amp -9 \\
                2 \amp 6 \amp -1 \amp 6 \\
                1 \amp 3 \amp -1 \amp 2
              \end{array} \right]
              \amp \xrightarrow{r_3 \leftrightarrow r_1}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 3 \amp -1 \amp 2 \\
                2 \amp 6 \amp -1 \amp 6 \\
                -3 \amp -9 \amp 1 \amp -9
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow[3r_1 + r_3]{-2r_1 + r_2}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 3 \amp -1 \amp 2 \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 2 \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp -2 \amp -3
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{2r_2 + r_3}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 3 \amp -1 \amp 2 \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 2 \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1
              \end{array} \right].
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          The last row says
          <md>
            0x_1 + 0x_2 + 0x_3 = 1, \qquad \text{or} \qquad 0 = 1,
          </md>
          which is impossible, and hence the system is inconsistent (no solution).
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-no-solution-planes">
          <caption>
            Each pair of planes intersects in a line, but the three planes have no
            common point. A solution must satisfy all three equations at once.
            The left panel shows finite portions of the infinite planes. The right
            panel fixes <m>x_1=0</m> to show three lines with three distinct pairwise
            crossings. The labels identify the equations in their original order.
          </caption>
          <image source="images/ch1-no-solution-planes.svg" width="90%">
            <shortdescription>Three planes intersect pairwise in parallel lines and have no common point. A two-dimensional slice at x1=0 shows three separate pairwise crossings.</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </figure>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <remark xml:id="rem-inconsistency-test">
      <p>
        In general, a system is inconsistent exactly when the row-echelon form of its
        augmented matrix has a row of the form
        <m>\left[ \begin{array}{cccc|c} 0 \amp 0 \amp \cdots \amp 0 \amp d \end{array} \right]</m>
        with <m>d \neq 0</m>.
      </p>
    </remark>

    <example xml:id="eg-infinitely-many-rr">
      <title>A System with Infinitely Many Solutions</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Solve the system
          <md>
            <mrow>
              2x + y + 3z \amp = 1
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              2y - z + x \amp = 0
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              10z - 6y \amp = 2
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \amp
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                2 \amp 1 \amp 3 \amp 1 \\
                1 \amp 2 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\
                0 \amp -6 \amp 10 \amp 2
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{r_1 \leftrightarrow r_2}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 2 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\
                2 \amp 1 \amp 3 \amp 1 \\
                0 \amp -6 \amp 10 \amp 2
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{-2r_1 + r_2}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 2 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\
                0 \amp -3 \amp 5 \amp 1 \\
                0 \amp -6 \amp 10 \amp 2
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{-2r_2 + r_3}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 2 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\
                0 \amp -3 \amp 5 \amp 1 \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{-\frac{1}{3} r_2}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 2 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\
                0 \amp 1 \amp -\frac{5}{3} \amp -\frac{1}{3} \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{-2r_2 + r_1}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 0 \amp \frac{7}{3} \amp \frac{2}{3} \\
                0 \amp 1 \amp -\frac{5}{3} \amp -\frac{1}{3} \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0
              \end{array} \right].
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          Columns <m>1</m> and <m>2</m> are pivot columns, so <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> are
          basic variables, whereas <m>z</m> is a free variable. Setting <m>z = s</m>, the
          two nonzero rows read
          <md>
            <mrow>
              x + \tfrac{7}{3}s \amp = \tfrac{2}{3}
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              y - \tfrac{5}{3}s \amp = -\tfrac{1}{3}
            </mrow>
          </md>
          and therefore the solution set in parametric form is
          <md>
            <mrow>
              x \amp = \tfrac{2}{3} - \tfrac{7}{3}s
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              y \amp = -\tfrac{1}{3} + \tfrac{5}{3}s
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              z \amp = s, \qquad \text{where } s \in \R.
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-infinitely-many-planes">
          <caption>All three planes contain the line <m>x=2/3-7s/3</m>, <m>y=-1/3+5s/3</m>, <m>z=s</m>. Every point on this line solves all three equations. The drawing shows finite portions of infinite planes;
          the labels identify the equations in their original order.</caption>
          <image source="images/ch1-infinitely-many-planes.svg" width="90%">
            <shortdescription>Three planes share the entire line x=2/3-7s/3, y=-1/3+5s/3, z=s.</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </figure>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <remark xml:id="rem-free-variables-count">
      <p>
        The pattern is now clear. If a system is consistent, then it has a unique solution
        when there are no free variables, and infinitely many solutions when there is at
        least one free variable <mdash /> one parameter for each free variable.
      </p>
    </remark>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-systems-with-parameters">
    <title>Systems Depending on Parameters</title>

    <p>
      A parameter can play two different roles. In a solution formula such as
      <m>z=s</m>, it describes the different solutions of one fixed system. Here,
      <m>a,b,c</m> instead specify the equations themselves: choosing their values
      selects a particular system. Treat them as fixed constants while solving,
      and check when an expression is zero before dividing by it.
    </p>

    <example xml:id="eg-parameters-abc">
      <title>Conditions on the Coefficients</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find all values of <m>a</m>, <m>b</m> and <m>c</m> (or conditions on <m>a</m>,
          <m>b</m> and <m>c</m>) so that the system
          <md>
            <mrow>
              2x + 3y + az \amp = b
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \phantom{2x + {}} -y + 2z \amp = c
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              x + 3y - 2z \amp = 1
            </mrow>
          </md>
          has (i) a unique solution, (ii) no solutions, and (iii) infinitely many
          solutions. In (i) and (iii), find the solution(s).
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Row reduce the augmented matrix, treating <m>a</m>, <m>b</m>, <m>c</m> as
          constants:
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                2 \amp 3 \amp a \amp b \\
                0 \amp -1 \amp 2 \amp c \\
                1 \amp 3 \amp -2 \amp 1
              \end{array} \right]
              \amp \xrightarrow{r_1 \leftrightarrow r_3}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 3 \amp -2 \amp 1 \\
                0 \amp -1 \amp 2 \amp c \\
                2 \amp 3 \amp a \amp b
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{-2r_1 + r_3}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 3 \amp -2 \amp 1 \\
                0 \amp -1 \amp 2 \amp c \\
                0 \amp -3 \amp a+4 \amp b-2
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{-3r_2 + r_3}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 3 \amp -2 \amp 1 \\
                0 \amp -1 \amp 2 \amp c \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp a-2 \amp b-2-3c
              \end{array} \right].
            </mrow>
          </md>
          Everything now hinges on the entry <m>a - 2</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>Case (I): <m>a - 2 \neq 0</m>, i.e., <m>a \neq 2</m>.</term>
          We may divide the third row by <m>a-2</m> and continue. Writing
          <m>K = \dfrac{b-2-3c}{a-2}</m> for brevity,
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{\left( \frac{1}{a-2} \right) r_3}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 3 \amp -2 \amp 1 \\
                0 \amp -1 \amp 2 \amp c \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp K
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{-r_2}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 3 \amp -2 \amp 1 \\
                0 \amp 1 \amp -2 \amp -c \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp K
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{2r_3+r_2}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 3 \amp -2 \amp 1 \\
                0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp -c + 2K \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp K
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{r_1 + 2r_3}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 3 \amp 0 \amp 1 + 2K \\
                0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp -c + 2K \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp K
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{-3r_2 + r_1}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 + 3c - 4K \\
                0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp -c + 2K \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp K
              \end{array} \right].
            </mrow>
          </md>
          Hence when <m>a \neq 2</m>, there is a unique solution:
          <md>
            <mrow>
              x \amp = 1 + 3c - 4\left( \frac{b-2-3c}{a-2} \right)
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              y \amp = -c + 2 \left( \frac{b-2-3c}{a-2} \right)
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              z \amp = \frac{b-2-3c}{a-2}.
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>Case (II): <m>a - 2 = 0</m> and <m>b - 2 - 3c \neq 0</m>.</term>
          The row-echelon matrix is
          <md>
            \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
              1 \amp 3 \amp -2 \amp 1 \\
              0 \amp -1 \amp 2 \amp c \\
              0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp b-2-3c
            \end{array} \right],
          </md>
          whose last row asserts <m>0 = b-2-3c \neq 0</m>. Then the system has no solution.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>Case (III): <m>a - 2 = 0</m> and <m>b - 3c = 2</m>.</term>
          Now the last row is entirely zero, and <m>z</m> is a free variable, so the
          system has infinitely many solutions. Setting <m>z = s</m>, the second row gives
          <m>-y + 2s = c</m>, so <m>y = -c + 2s</m>, and the first row gives
          <md>
            x = 1 - 3y + 2z = 1 - 3(-c + 2s) + 2s = 1 + 3c - 4s.
          </md>
          The solution set in parametric form is
          <md>
            <mrow>
              x \amp = 1 + 3c - 4s
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              y \amp = -c + 2s
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              z \amp = s, \qquad \text{where } s \in \R.
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>
  </subsection>

  <exercises xml:id="exercises-ch1-reduction">
    <title>Practice: Interpreting Row Reduction</title>
    <exercise xml:id="ex-ch1-zero-versus-contradiction">
      <statement><p>Translate the augmented rows
      <m>[\,0\;0\;0\mid0\,]</m> and <m>[\,0\;0\;0\mid5\,]</m> into equations.
      Which row proves inconsistency, and why?</p></statement>
      <answer><p>They assert <m>0=0</m> and <m>0=5</m>, respectively.
      The first imposes no restriction; the second is impossible and proves inconsistency.</p></answer>
    </exercise>
    <exercise xml:id="ex-ch1-two-free-variables">
      <statement><p>Solve <m>x_1+2x_2-x_3+x_4=3</m>, <m>x_2+x_4=1</m>.
      Identify the basic and free variables, use one parameter for each free variable,
      and verify your answer in both equations.</p></statement>
      <answer><p>The reduced augmented matrix is
      <m>\left[\begin{array}{cccc|c}1\amp0\amp-1\amp-1\amp1\\0\amp1\amp0\amp1\amp1\end{array}\right]</m>.
      Basic variables: <m>x_1,x_2</m>; free variables: <m>x_3,x_4</m>.
      All solutions are <m>x_1=1+s+t</m>, <m>x_2=1-t</m>, <m>x_3=s</m>, <m>x_4=t</m>,
      for arbitrary real <m>s,t</m>. Substitution gives
      <m>(1+s+t)+2(1-t)-s+t=3</m> and <m>(1-t)+t=1</m>.</p></answer>
    </exercise>
    <exercise xml:id="ex-ch1-zero-row-outcomes">
      <statement><p>A student says, <q>A zero row means infinitely many solutions.</q>
      Explain the error using these echelon augmented matrices:
      <md>
      \left[\begin{array}{cc|c}1\amp0\amp2\\0\amp1\amp3\\0\amp0\amp0\end{array}\right],
      \qquad
      \left[\begin{array}{cc|c}1\amp1\amp2\\0\amp0\amp0\end{array}\right],
      \qquad
      \left[\begin{array}{cc|c}1\amp1\amp2\\0\amp0\amp1\\0\amp0\amp0\end{array}\right].
      </md>Classify each system.</p></statement>
      <answer><p>The first has the unique solution <m>x_1=2,x_2=3</m>.
      The second has infinitely many solutions <m>x_1=2-s,x_2=s</m>.
      The third has no solution because of its contradictory second row.
      Check consistency first, then whether any variables are free.</p></answer>
    </exercise>
    <exercise xml:id="ex-ch1-parameter-division">
      <statement><p>A row reduces to <m>[\,0\;a-2\mid b\,]</m> in a two-variable
      system whose first row has a pivot in the first column. What cases must be
      considered before dividing by <m>a-2</m>?</p></statement>
      <answer><p>If <m>a\ne2</m>, division is allowed and the system has a unique solution.
      If <m>a=2,b\ne0</m>, it is inconsistent. If <m>a=2,b=0</m>, the second variable
      is free and there are infinitely many solutions.</p></answer>
    </exercise>
  </exercises>
</section>
